CloudBurrow patch 36 to github.com/goccy/googlesqlite v0.3.1
(cloudburrow/cloudburrow#1418). Applies after patch 0032.

The gap: BigQuery's APPENDS and CHANGES table functions read a table's
history of changes, each row inserted, deleted or updated (a DELETE of
the old row with _CHANGE_IS_FOR_UPDATE and an UPDATE of the new), with
the commit time of the change in _CHANGE_TIMESTAMP
(https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/time-series-functions).
SQLite keeps no history: FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF reads a table only as it
is now.

The patch gives every table a CREATE TABLE makes (not a temporary one,
not a view, and named project.dataset.table, so not the emulator's own
metadata tables, nor one of CloudBurrow's _cloudburrow datasets, nor
one with a column named _CHANGE_...) a change table, `project.dataset.table$changes`: the
table's columns (not its pseudo-columns) and _CHANGE_TYPE STRING,
_CHANGE_TIMESTAMP TIMESTAMP and _CHANGE_IS_FOR_UPDATE BOOL, filled by
SQLite triggers AFTER INSERT, DELETE and UPDATE on the table. Every
write reaches a table by SQLite INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE (DML, MERGE,
TRUNCATE TABLE; the emulator's tabledata.insertAll, loads, Storage Write
API, WRITE_TRUNCATE and query destinations), so the triggers see them
all. The change time is the time of the transaction's first change, the
same for every row it writes, kept per connection until a commit or a
rollback; an UPDATE of a row the same transaction inserted (the
emulator's rounding triggers, bigquery-emulator patch 0020) is part of
the INSERT record. A CREATE TABLE ... AS SELECT's rows are recorded as inserted
when it is made; DROP TABLE drops the change table; CREATE OR REPLACE
TABLE starts it again; CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS of a table that is
there keeps it, in the catalog or not (CloudBurrow's front takes a table
out of the catalog and adds it again to put it in step after a failed
script). A change older than seven days (BigQuery's longest time
travel window) is deleted as each new change is recorded. No BigQuery
table id has a `$`, a wildcard leaves change tables out, and so does
INFORMATION_SCHEMA. CloudBurrow's front reads them for APPENDS and
CHANGES. change_history_test.go fails without the patch and passes with
it.

